{
  "title": "Adamantinoma",
  "url": "https://www.orthobullets.com/pathology/8048/adamantinoma",
  "subtitle": "Updated: Jun 22 2021",
  "sections": [
    {
      "text": "summary",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Adamantinomas are rare, low-grade malignant tumors of unknown etiology that are almost always located in the diaphysis of the mid-tibia.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "5",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "The condition usually presents in patients who are between 20 and 40 years old with regional pain and a palpable mass.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Diagnosis is made with a biopsy showing nests of epithelial-like cells arranged in palisading or glandular pattern, in a background of fibrous stroma.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "4",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Treatment is usually wide-margin surgical resection.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "1",
          "children": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Epidemiology",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Incidence",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "less than 300 cases have been documented",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Demographics",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "occurs in young adults (20 - 40 years of age)",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Anatomic location",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "almost always located in **mid-tibia**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Etiology",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Pathophysiology",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "unknown",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Associated conditions",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "**osteofibrous dysplasia**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "historically, it was thought that osteofibrous dysplasia (OFD) was a precursor to this adamantinoma, however current studies have cast doubt on this theory",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Presentation",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Symptoms",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "**pain** of months to years duration",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Physical exam",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "**bowing deformity** or a **palpable mass** of tibia is common",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Imaging",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Radiographs",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "**multiple sharply circumscribed lucent lesions (\"soap bubble\" appearance)** with interspersed sclerotic bone in mid-tibia",
              "table": null,
              "images": [
                {
                  "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/8048/images/Case%20B%20-%20tibia%20-%20xray%20-%20parsons_moved.png",
                  "caption": ""
                }
              ],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "some lesions may destroy cortex",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "may see bowing of the tibia",
              "table": null,
              "images": [
                {
                  "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/8048/images/Case%20A%20-%20tibia%20-%20xray%20b%20-%20parsons_moved.jpg",
                  "caption": ""
                }
              ],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "**radiographic evolution of lesions** is helpful in the diagnosis as lesions may continue to grow and erode thru the cortex",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "unlike other primary bone tumors, adamantinoma typically shows no periosteal reaction",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Studies",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Histology",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "**biphasic**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "contains both epithelial and fibrous mesenchymal cells",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "**nests of epithelial-like cells** arranged in palisading or **glandular pattern**",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [
                    {
                      "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/8048/images/adamantinoma%20histo.jpg",
                      "caption": ""
                    },
                    {
                      "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/8048/images/Histology%20A_moved.jpg",
                      "caption": ""
                    }
                  ],
                  "importance": "5",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "stain for keratin",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "4",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": "background of **fibrous stroma**",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [
                    {
                      "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/8048/images/Histology%20B%20-%20mod%20power%20-%20parsons_moved.png",
                      "caption": ""
                    }
                  ],
                  "importance": "5",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Differential",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Osteofibrous dysplasia",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "differentiating between osteofibrous dysplasia and adamantinoma is critical",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "osteofibrous dysplasia is benign and treated with observation",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "adamantinoma is malignant and treat with surgical resection",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "",
          "table": [
            {
